WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.430 --> 00:00:05.832
[JINGLE PLAYS]

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:14.400
There is a
bs_theme_preview function

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:17.580
that, given that
you don't already

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:20.430
have a Shiny
application that you

00:00:20.430 --> 00:00:26.070
want to preview a theme for, you
can give me bs_theme_preview--

00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:30.240
like, if I already had
in mind like a theme

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:35.730
that I wanted to use and didn't
want to do the work of actually

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:45.650
programming a Shiny app example,
I can just save this theme here

00:00:45.650 --> 00:00:58.660
and use this bs_theme_preview,
give it a theme,

00:00:58.660 --> 00:01:04.030
and it will apply that
theme to this Shiny demo

00:01:04.030 --> 00:01:09.880
app that kind of tries its best
to stuff all of the core Shiny

00:01:09.880 --> 00:01:15.790
UI stuff that we support
into this example app.

00:01:15.790 --> 00:01:17.890
So you can see how
those styles would

00:01:17.890 --> 00:01:22.870
end up affecting slider input,
and selectize input, and date

00:01:22.870 --> 00:01:29.350
input, as well as things
like verbatim text output

00:01:29.350 --> 00:01:34.000
and all of the sort
of Shiny widgets

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:38.410
that you may or may
not be already used to.

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:43.630
And then it also,
at the same time,

00:01:43.630 --> 00:01:49.750
overlays this interactive
widget that I can drag around

00:01:49.750 --> 00:01:59.310
and use to make tweaks to
the actual theme settings.

00:01:59.310 --> 00:02:02.610
So I can change from
something closer

00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:06.750
to a black to something closer
to like an ice blue theme.

00:02:09.699 --> 00:02:12.340
So it's got the two main
background and foreground

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:12.920
colors.

00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:17.680
It's also got the ability
to use a Bootswatch

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:20.890
theme if you want to.

00:02:20.890 --> 00:02:24.850
Just be aware, if you
use a Bootswatch theme,

00:02:24.850 --> 00:02:28.960
don't expect the custom
theming options to always

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:31.610
work super great with it.

00:02:31.610 --> 00:02:34.960
So just want to set
expectations there,

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:39.340
that just because of the way
that Bootswatch is currently

00:02:39.340 --> 00:02:42.910
set up, they haven't implemented
those things well enough

00:02:42.910 --> 00:02:49.150
in mine to allow you to
tweak them in ways that work

00:02:49.150 --> 00:02:54.050
as well as just using the
default bootstrap theme.

00:02:54.050 --> 00:02:57.920
So just be aware of that.

00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:01.060
So you can change the main
background-foreground, as well

00:03:01.060 --> 00:03:07.410
as I could have a different
primary color here.

00:03:07.410 --> 00:03:10.890
That will change
tabsetPanel, things

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:16.680
like check boxes and radio
buttons, slider inputs also.

00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:22.620
We try our best to use
those like main semantics

00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:27.360
of bootstrap and propagate them
throughout all of the widgets

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:28.830
that we provide in Shiny.

00:03:32.090 --> 00:03:37.670
So the main like color and
Bootswatch settings, as well as

00:03:37.670 --> 00:03:38.450
fonts.

00:03:38.450 --> 00:03:41.930
And again, this has
integration with Google Fonts,

00:03:41.930 --> 00:03:49.130
where I could choose any sans
serif font that I wanted.

00:03:49.130 --> 00:03:51.380
Let's choose something like--

00:03:54.022 --> 00:03:57.100
I'm not sure how to pronounce
this one, but I do like it--

00:03:57.100 --> 00:04:00.290
Monserrat.

00:04:00.290 --> 00:04:02.840
So I could change from
prompt to Montserrat.

00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:07.890
Oh, and actually,
that might be a bug,

00:04:07.890 --> 00:04:12.690
that it seems to have gone back
to my original theme settings.

00:04:12.690 --> 00:04:16.820
But now you can
see, at the top here

00:04:16.820 --> 00:04:19.760
and on the tabsetPanel, that's
got a slightly different

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:22.640
looking for here.

00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:25.840
It also has Thematic
enabled, so that we

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:29.110
can look at how the
theme would actually

00:04:29.110 --> 00:04:34.460
be propagated to rplots
defaults settings as well.

00:04:34.460 --> 00:04:38.900
So I could, again, change
to a blue color here,

00:04:38.900 --> 00:04:42.760
and that will just automatically
kind of propagate to drplot.

00:04:45.910 --> 00:04:47.800
Same thing with DT tables.

00:04:47.800 --> 00:04:50.230
DT also has some
nice integration

00:04:50.230 --> 00:04:55.120
with bslib, where it
derives its sort of accent

00:04:55.120 --> 00:04:58.870
colors, and main colors,
and fonts as well--

00:04:58.870 --> 00:05:05.010
all those styles--
from the bsloop theme.

00:05:05.010 --> 00:05:08.160
And then these are
just some other things

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:11.340
that we provide in Shiny
UI, like modals and progress

00:05:11.340 --> 00:05:15.530
bars, notifications.

00:05:15.530 --> 00:05:19.900
And then the difference between
how the heading fonts would

00:05:19.900 --> 00:05:24.560
look versus code and
base font, and then

00:05:24.560 --> 00:05:28.820
some other kind of more
low-level options for more

00:05:28.820 --> 00:05:30.140
advanced themeing.

00:05:30.140 --> 00:05:37.160
So you can see in my R console
here various output that

00:05:37.160 --> 00:05:42.080
has been essentially
echoed as I've made changes

00:05:42.080 --> 00:05:44.270
to the theming application.

00:05:44.270 --> 00:05:47.480
And it should do a
good job of keeping

00:05:47.480 --> 00:05:52.890
track of just the changes that
you've made interactively.

00:05:52.890 --> 00:05:55.730
So this captures
the fact that I've

00:05:55.730 --> 00:05:59.810
changed to more of a blue
background and foreground

00:05:59.810 --> 00:06:05.400
color, as well as the
Monserrat font setting,

00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:12.180
and it's not quite smart enough
to know that it should actually

00:06:12.180 --> 00:06:15.120
integrate this stuff
with the input theme

00:06:15.120 --> 00:06:16.360
that you've given it.

00:06:16.360 --> 00:06:21.870
So you will have to essentially
copy this code here.

00:06:21.870 --> 00:06:25.740
And it does make the assumption
that your theme is named theme.

00:06:25.740 --> 00:06:29.520
But thankfully I've actually
used the name theme.

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:33.780
So I can just say
bs_theme_update this theme

00:06:33.780 --> 00:06:35.290
with these new settings.

00:06:35.290 --> 00:06:43.110
And now, if I feed this into
input again on the BSD preview,

00:06:43.110 --> 00:06:47.880
it'll use the
Montserrat settings.

00:06:47.880 --> 00:06:49.620
The bs_theme_preview
is really nice

00:06:49.620 --> 00:06:53.310
if, again, you don't
already have a Shiny

00:06:53.310 --> 00:06:55.230
app that you're
using, and you want

00:06:55.230 --> 00:07:00.590
to get a feel for
what colors and fonts

00:07:00.590 --> 00:07:03.230
you might want to use in
your future Shiny app.

00:07:03.230 --> 00:07:06.080
But if you already have
an existing Shiny app,

00:07:06.080 --> 00:07:11.040
you can leverage that same
interactive theming widget.

00:07:11.040 --> 00:07:19.475
So if I already have this
navbar page here, I can--

00:07:22.120 --> 00:07:27.580
first, let me actually do more
of a traditional looking set

00:07:27.580 --> 00:07:29.520
up.

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:33.106
Here's the server function.

00:07:33.106 --> 00:07:37.485
It has the input and output
arguments, as well as session.

00:07:40.070 --> 00:07:44.780
And then, bslib has a
function called bs_themer.

00:07:44.780 --> 00:07:52.530
Diemer that you can call
inside of your server function,

00:07:52.530 --> 00:08:02.130
and then it will overlay
that same interactive themer

00:08:02.130 --> 00:08:06.350
on this particular Shiny app.

00:08:06.350 --> 00:08:10.550
So now I could go in and do the
same thing to my basic navbnar

00:08:10.550 --> 00:08:17.790
page and still get the same
experience of the code being

00:08:17.790 --> 00:08:20.085
put to replicate the changes.

00:08:22.620 --> 00:08:24.360
And you don't necessarily--

00:08:24.360 --> 00:08:25.770
you just need to call this once.

00:08:25.770 --> 00:08:27.420
You don't need to
worry about calling

00:08:27.420 --> 00:08:30.500
this in a Reactive context
or anything like that.

00:08:30.500 --> 00:08:33.570
If you want to do this, just
go to the top of your server

00:08:33.570 --> 00:08:39.059
function, and call
this function once,

00:08:39.059 --> 00:08:42.270
and then they will
just overlay that.

00:08:42.270 --> 00:08:49.020
And you can also collapse it if
you don't actually really want

00:08:49.020 --> 00:08:52.800
to use it and you want
to do some other stuff

00:08:52.800 --> 00:08:53.640
with your Shiny app.

00:08:53.640 --> 00:08:57.250
You can collapse it and kind
of get it out of your way

00:08:57.250 --> 00:08:59.040
If you don't want it anymore.

00:08:59.040 --> 00:09:01.350
And yeah, not only can
you do this with Shiny,

00:09:01.350 --> 00:09:05.490
but you can also do this with
your R Markdown documents.

00:09:05.490 --> 00:09:09.880
But there is-- it's a little
bit more tricky to figure out

00:09:09.880 --> 00:09:13.120
how to do that, in
the sense that it

00:09:13.120 --> 00:09:15.650
requires two different things.

00:09:15.650 --> 00:09:23.050
It requires you to add a
runtime Shiny to your R Markdown

00:09:23.050 --> 00:09:25.780
document, because
really we can't

00:09:25.780 --> 00:09:30.160
get the full features of
this interactive theming

00:09:30.160 --> 00:09:32.035
widget working without
a Shiny runtime.

00:09:34.780 --> 00:09:41.740
And then, as well,
in your setup chunk,

00:09:41.740 --> 00:09:44.050
you want to call this bs_themer.

00:09:44.050 --> 00:09:47.620
Sort of like how you called
the bs_themer in the server

00:09:47.620 --> 00:09:52.780
function, you want to do the
same thing in code that's going

00:09:52.780 --> 00:09:58.650
to run in a server context,
which I can't remember

00:09:58.650 --> 00:10:01.410
if that's--

00:10:01.410 --> 00:10:03.750
well, actually, looks
like that worked.

00:10:03.750 --> 00:10:08.550
So we got this original example
that we're working from,

00:10:08.550 --> 00:10:10.270
and now this interactive themer.

00:10:10.270 --> 00:10:13.650
So if I wanted to
have a look at one

00:10:13.650 --> 00:10:21.210
of these prepackaged
themes, say Minty,

00:10:21.210 --> 00:10:24.900
I can see how that will look
on my R Markdown document.

00:10:27.420 --> 00:10:33.750
Unfortunately, Thematic won't
work if you're statically

00:10:33.750 --> 00:10:35.730
rendering these plots.

00:10:35.730 --> 00:10:37.860
So it doesn't know that--

00:10:37.860 --> 00:10:40.890
by default, it won't
know to re-render itself

00:10:40.890 --> 00:10:43.110
when your theme has changed.

00:10:43.110 --> 00:10:45.330
But you can make
that work as well,

00:10:45.330 --> 00:10:51.600
if you instead
dynamically render this

00:10:51.600 --> 00:10:55.050
through a renderPlot.

00:10:55.050 --> 00:10:56.700
Which now we can do.

00:10:56.700 --> 00:10:58.770
Because we have
the runtime Shiny,

00:10:58.770 --> 00:11:04.990
we can have the ability to
render R plots dynamically.

00:11:04.990 --> 00:11:09.090
So let's just make
sure this works

00:11:09.090 --> 00:11:12.375
by stopping and running again.

00:11:16.500 --> 00:11:17.000
All right.

00:11:17.000 --> 00:11:19.850
So it's recognized
the default theme.

00:11:19.850 --> 00:11:27.080
But now, if I
change to Minty, it

00:11:27.080 --> 00:11:31.250
will know to re-render itself
with those new CSS settings

00:11:31.250 --> 00:11:33.400
essentially.

